        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Changes in diet, lifestyle, antibiotic usage, and the environmental stresses during space flight can alter crewmembers' intestinal microbiome. It has been observed that after two weeks of space flight there is a significant decline in the number of bacterial species in the intestine, simultaneous with immune dysregulation and a diminished response to infection. As mission distance and duration increase, the need grows for non-invasive disease prevention and immunomodulation, especially given the limited medical response capability expected for these missions. Based on previous studies that demonstrated enhanced microbiological responses to the low shear environment of space flight, the induction of enhanced beneficial responses could logically be expected by probiotic organisms that have demonstrated beneficial interactions with human immune cells. Understanding the microgravity effects on the stress tolerances and genetic expression of probiotic bacteria can enable optimization of growth, survival, strain selection, and conferred benefits in space flight. 
<p>
The probiotic <i>Lactobacillus acidophilus</i> has been selected to begin these probiotic studies due to its wide commercial use and documented benefits that include inhibition of virulence related gene expression in pathogens and mucosal stimulation of immune cells. The objective of this study is to characterize the effect of the low shear modeled microgravity (LSMMG) culture environment in the rotating wall vessel (RWV) on the growth, stress response, and transcriptomic response of L. acidophilus. Investigators hypothesize that L. acidophilus will maintain or improve its growth and stress response following culture in the LSMMG environment, and that transcriptomic analysis will define the associated molecular mechanism(s), resulting in the ability to optimize strain selection. 
<p>
Specific Aim: Determine the effects of LSMMG culture on the growth, stress response, and transcriptomic profile of L. acidophilus ATCC 4356.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Ground-based characterization will be accomplished using the low shear modeled microgravity (LSMMG) culture environment in the rotating wall vessel (RWV). Cells cultured within the RWV are maintained in suspension and experience very low levels of fluid shear over their surface, similar to what is expected in microgravity. Numerous investigations have revealed parallel cellular responses to culture within the RWV with liquid culture in spaceflight. Additionally, mathematical modeling has demonstrated that the low levels of fluid shear created by the RWV are physiologically relevant to sites within the human body and provide a more accurate account of the state of probiotic organisms at the brush border of intestinal epithelial cells than would be obtained with standard culture. It is expected that probiotics will be delivered in a rehydratable food product in spaceflight, so liquid culture in an RWV is a logical analog to investigate potential probiotic responses to space flight and at human epithelial sites.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is in progress.
<p>
